[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The suspected component is the physical WAN link between router0 and router1 (interfaces eth2 on both routers). If this link had failed, Layer-1/2 would show DOWN/LOWER_DOWN, ARP would be unresolved, and L3 pings between 10.16.1.1/24 and 10.16.1.2/24 would fail. Instead, on router0 eth2 is UP/LOWER_UP with 10.16.1.1/24; ethtool reports Link detected: yes (10 G, full duplex). On router1, eth2 is likewise UP/LOWER_UP with 10.16.1.2/24; ethtool shows Link detected: yes. Bidirectional pings succeed with 0% loss and sub-millisecond RTTs from router0->10.16.1.2 and router1->10.16.1.1. ARP/neighbor tables confirm reachability (router0 shows 10.16.1.2 lladdr ce:dc:12:…, router1 shows 10.16.1.1 lladdr ba:7a:…).

Mechanism: The presence of carrier (LOWER_UP), successful L2 neighbor resolution, and clean L3 echo replies conclusively indicate the physical link is intact and passing traffic. Localization: The tested link is wan_link_r0_r1, realized as router0.eth2 (10.16.1.1/24) to router1.eth2 (10.16.1.2/24).

Root cause: There is no physical failure on the router0<->router1 link; the suspected cable/serial drop did not occur.

Corrective fix: Do not replace the cable or shut/no shut this link. Focus follow-up troubleshooting on routing/control-plane changes made on router1 during the change window (e.g., route redistribution, filtering, or policy affecting inter-zone prefixes). Verify dynamic routing adjacencies and RIB entries, and revert or correct the change as needed. The r0–r1 physical interfaces require no corrective action.
=== END ===